{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"458e3a53\" lines=\"19-32\">\n<description>\nExtract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.\n- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.\n\nRule\n- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.\n- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.\n\nKey insight\n- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2f767503\" lines=\"66-81\">\n<description>\nErase each target-color object crossed by the ray pointed by the bar-and-control marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There is one three-pixel straight marker bar and one singleton control pixel adjacent to the bar center.\n- The remaining non-background color forms multiple 4-connected target objects.\n\nRule:\n- Cast a straight ray from the marker bar center in the direction away from the adjacent control pixel.\n- Find every 4-connected target-color object that intersects that ray.\n- Recolor all pixels of those hit target objects to the background; all other pixels stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The whole object is erased when the center ray touches any of its pixels; the full marker bar width is not swept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6e50e54\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nMove each maroon marker toward the blue rectangle and recolor the rectangle red.\n\nInput structure\nA background canvas contains one solid blue rectangular object and several isolated\nmaroon marker pixels. Each marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the\nrectangle and lies outside it.\n\nRule\nStart with a background canvas, repaint the blue rectangle red, and move each\nmaroon marker straight toward the rectangle along its row or column. Exactly one\nmarker is strictly closer to the rectangle than all others and enters the\nrectangle boundary; every other marker lands in the adjacent exterior cell just\noutside its aligned side.\n\nKey insight\nThe single entering marker is the unique global nearest marker to the rectangle,\nnot the nearest marker on each of the four sides.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d255d7a7\" lines=\"99-114\">\n<description>\nMove the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.\n- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.\n\nRule\n- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.\n- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.\n- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22806e14\" lines=\"23-37\">\n<description>\nMark the center of each odd-sized solid square with the plus marker color, erasing the marker only when it is outside the marked-center field.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is background.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: one unique 3x3 plus marker and one color forming separated solid square objects.\n\nRule:\n- For every non-marker 4-connected object, assert it is a solid square.\n- If the square side length is odd, recolor its center pixel to the plus marker color; even-sided squares stay unchanged.\n- Preserve the plus marker when its center lies inside the bounding box of the odd-square centers; otherwise erase it to background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Odd-sided solid squares have a single visible center pixel, and the plus supplies the color used to identify those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b80bb43\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nRemove diagonal staircase noise and complete broken straight lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; one foreground color fg forming axis-aligned lines.\n- Some fg pixels form 45° diagonal chains (8-connected but 4-disconnected from lines).\n\nRule:\n- Delete all diagonal-only fg pixels (those with no orthogonal fg neighbor, only diagonal ones).\n- Fill short gaps in horizontal/vertical lines where a deleted diagonal was nearby.\n- Prune remaining diagonal-adjacent leaf pixels near former diagonal sites.\n\nKey insight: Diagonal pixels are hints marking where a straight line is broken; their span sets the maximum gap size to fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bae5c565\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nErase the top palette row and redraw it below as a widening wedge centered on its missing cell.\n\nInput structure\n- The top row is a palette row with exactly one background gap.\n- Below it, the only foreground is one vertical marker bar of a single color.\n- The marker bar is either in the gap column or immediately beside it.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to background.\n- Starting at the top of the marker bar, each next row copies one more\n  column of the top-row palette on both sides of the gap.\n- Fill the gap column with the marker color, and keep the vertical marker bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The lone background cell in the top row is the center of the expanding\n  copy; the lower vertical bar provides the center color and the row where\n  the expansion begins.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a09f6c25\" lines=\"33-50\">\n<description>\nRecolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:\n  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.\n- Single red pixels are noise.\n\nRule:\n- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.\n- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects\n  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.\n- Leave isolated red pixels as background.\n\nKey insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a\nlabel for the object's reflection symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5545f144\" lines=\"191-207\">\n<description>\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.\n- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the rightmost frame.\n- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable\n  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the\n  nearest one.\n- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.\n- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.\n\nKey insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
